WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.008
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.008 --> 00:00:09.810
Silicon Valley is the new American dream.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.531
It's where companies are born,
and millionaires are made.

00:00:12.531 --> 00:00:16.430
Like many of you,
I was part of that dream.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.400
Before coming to the GSB,
I was an Executive at Cisco, and

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.893
then went on to work for
Adventure Back Startup.

00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:26.960
I'd like to start with
a much simpler dream.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.679
The dream of my grandparents.

00:00:28.679 --> 00:00:31.156
Antonio Nicolasa.

00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:38.510
They dreamt of running water,
electricity, and indoor plumbing.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.874
Things they didn't have in
their small village in Mexico.

00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:46.750
So they moved to the United States,
and search of a better life.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.790
Where they became migrant farm workers
who harvested the fields right here

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:57.860
in Silicon Valley, before it
became Google, Apple and Facebook.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.070
But as you know, the fields disappeared.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:02.120
And when that happened,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.204
my family transitioned to roles to
provide the backbone of this valley.

00:01:05.204 --> 00:01:06.260
The people who clean your homes.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.430
The people who take out your trash.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.818
And the people who even
take care of your children.

00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:19.080
One morning before school, I got up really
early to go with my grandparents to work.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.628
I walked into the bathroom to
help my grandmother clean, and

00:01:23.628 --> 00:01:25.532
I'd never seen such a mess before.

00:01:25.532 --> 00:01:29.460
My grandmother saw the look on my face.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.260
And she explained how
important this job was,

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.230
that it would allow us to
put food on the table.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:37.420
She encouraged me to
take pride in my work,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.580
to make that bathroom as sparkling
clean as I could make it.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.763
My grandmother,
who had a fourth grade education,

00:01:46.763 --> 00:01:52.353
taught me that day to approach each and
every task with dignity and with grit.

00:01:54.985 --> 00:01:59.882
In Silicon Valley, venture capitalists
are in search of the next billion dollar

00:01:59.882 --> 00:02:01.910
startup, the elusive unicorn.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.490
Well, people from my neighborhood,

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.270
we have a very different
definition of what a unicorn is.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.019
I grew up in East San Jose,
about 20 miles south of here.

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:17.900
When I think of home, I think of
my grandmother's yellow kitchen.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.129
I think of the scent of
her homemade tortillas.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.335
And I think of the sounds
of Mariachi music.

00:02:24.335 --> 00:02:29.027
As a little girl, I loved to play outside.

00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:32.805
And one day, I managed to sneak
out when no one was looking and

00:02:32.805 --> 00:02:35.044
was happily playing in the garden.

00:02:35.044 --> 00:02:40.440
When out of the corner of my eye,
I saw my grandmother running towards me.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.110
She scooped me in her arms, and
like many Mexican grandmothers

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:48.585
scolded me in Spanish, and
then made the sign of the cross.

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:52.520
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You see,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.740
my grandmother knew that although
our neighborhood was very beautiful,

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.360
it could also be very dangerous.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.430
It meant walking home from school and
having a gun pulled on you.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:09.400
It meant being peer
pressured to join a gang.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.536
It meant having a baby,
when you were a baby.

00:03:15.448 --> 00:03:17.940
My family wasn't immuned to this, sadly.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.450
And of my 20 cousins,
only 10 graduated from high school,

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.360
and only 3 of us would go on
to graduate from college.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.907
This isn't unique to my family, but

00:03:31.907 --> 00:03:35.411
is happening to Latino
families across the country.

00:03:35.411 --> 00:03:40.964
Latinos with college degrees,
these are the unicorns.

00:03:44.877 --> 00:03:48.705
In Silicon Valley,
technology has been booming all around us.

00:03:48.705 --> 00:03:52.470
It's been creating tremendous opportunity
and wealth, it's been exciting.

00:03:55.304 --> 00:03:58.079
And my family has been able to take
part of the little piece of it.

00:03:59.828 --> 00:04:02.700
My younger brother Rikko is one
of the smartest guys I know.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.130
It would really frustrate me when I was
stuck on my physics homework assignment,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.010
and he would manage to
solve it effortlessly.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:16.310
He's the kind of guy who loved to take
things apart, and put them back together.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.891
He was a natural engineer.

00:04:17.891 --> 00:04:21.360
My brother is also a high school dropout.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.491
And he became a dad when
he was 17 years old.

00:04:25.491 --> 00:04:31.500
My brother was able to turn his
life around, to go back to school.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.392
And find a job working in manufacturing
for a local tech company.

00:04:34.392 --> 00:04:39.144
Because of that opportunity,
he was able not only to provide for

00:04:39.144 --> 00:04:41.170
his family but also for me.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:45.760
And one summer,
my brother gave me his paychecks,

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.890
so that I could live in Paris and
study at the Sorbonne.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:56.938
That simple act of generosity,
and love from a different track,

00:04:56.938 --> 00:05:01.452
it's the reason that I'm
here with all of you.

00:05:01.452 --> 00:05:08.000
But even with that college degree,
my path to career and technology.

00:05:09.555 --> 00:05:15.125
With very different in the part that
many of you took, it was a hustle.

00:05:15.125 --> 00:05:18.983
I worked at three different temporary
agencies before I landed that first job

00:05:18.983 --> 00:05:20.175
at Cisco.

00:05:20.175 --> 00:05:26.135
And I will never forget receiving that
offer letter, and being so excited.

00:05:26.135 --> 00:05:29.227
Not only what it meant for me,
but what it meant for my family.

00:05:34.764 --> 00:05:35.670
But the bubble burst.

00:05:38.381 --> 00:05:40.750
The manufacturing jobs went away.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.568
My brother lost his job.

00:05:43.568 --> 00:05:46.493
Other people in my family lost their jobs.

00:05:48.872 --> 00:05:52.294
My parents couldn't afford the home
anymore, so they looked to me,

00:05:52.294 --> 00:05:54.069
the one with the good job, to help.

00:05:54.069 --> 00:06:00.640
That entry level salary at Cisco,
barely covered a third of their mortgage.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:07.757
So my parents moved to central California,
back to the fields, where it all began.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.100
The Silicon Valley dream?

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:13.587
It was out of reach for them.

00:06:16.775 --> 00:06:20.390
While this area has been
growing at record phase.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:25.790
Communities like east San Jose have been
suffering one setback after the other.

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:30.702
Loss of jobs, loss of homes and
lack of upward mobility.

00:06:33.561 --> 00:06:36.000
This isn't just my problem.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.000
It's our problem.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.079
Latinos represent one of the fastest
growing groups in the country.

00:06:41.079 --> 00:06:47.239
This is expected to project
a $1.5 trillion opportunity.

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:48.460
Think about that.

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:55.240
I bet you didn't know that Latinos have
this beautiful entrepreneurial spirit.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:00.090
They start businesses at record rate.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:04.071
Within my family alone, there have been
five business that have been started.

00:07:04.071 --> 00:07:09.530
So why, if Latinos are starting
businesses at record rate.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:13.160
Why are we able to capitalize on them?

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:16.420
Professor here at the GSP,
has been setting this

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:20.690
very phenomenon as part of the Standford
Latino Entrepreneurship Initiative.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.187
And one of the things
that he's discovered,

00:07:23.187 --> 00:07:25.165
is Latinos lack access to mentors.

00:07:26.872 --> 00:07:30.734
As one of the few people from my
neighborhood who had a career in

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:34.010
technology, I knew how
important it was to go back.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.070
So I went to the schools I attended and
volunteered.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:41.100
I hosted events at local libraries.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.440
I even started a small scholarship fund.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.260
Through this work, I met Carlos.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.322
Carlos had just finished his
freshman year at Harvard.

00:07:51.322 --> 00:07:55.930
When I asked Carlos what his
plans were for the summer.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:08:00.340
He looked at me and said that he was
going to work construction with his dad.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.196
When I asked, Carlos,
have you thought of an internship?

00:08:03.196 --> 00:08:09.782
He said, I would love an internship,
but I have no idea where to begin.

00:08:09.782 --> 00:08:13.590
Carlos was one of the lucky ones.

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:16.490
I wanted the leading
institutions in this country.

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:20.420
And yet, even he was struggling to
navigate this business landscape.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:23.460
I knew how that felt.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:28.462
But more importantly, I knew how
to help them find that internship.

00:08:28.462 --> 00:08:32.316
People like Carlos may not
have the polished resume.

00:08:32.316 --> 00:08:35.690
They may not have the pedigree.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:40.600
But what do they have, is dignity,
grit, and their entrepreneurial spirit.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.488
What they're missing, is opportunity.

00:08:45.466 --> 00:08:50.393
So, what I'd like to ask
each of you is that,

00:08:50.393 --> 00:08:56.380
you either are,
all will become leaders in business.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:59.970
In this valley and across the world.

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:03.715
Please look again at people
from a different path.

00:09:03.715 --> 00:09:06.978
Hire them, mentor them.

00:09:06.978 --> 00:09:11.609
Imagine the impact that all of
us could have on communities

00:09:11.609 --> 00:09:16.344
like east San Jose,
if we add opportunity to the equation.

00:09:18.547 --> 00:09:19.140
Thank you.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:25.820
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:29.819
[MUSIC]

